// NOT A DIVERGENCE — values agree in both modes. A CODEGEN DEFECT
// worth fixing: a narrow-int slice bound or bytes index makes cranelift
// reject the whole enclosing kernel with a VERIFIER error, so an
// arbitrarily large region silently falls back to the node-walk.
//
//   graphix --fusion-stats 00_u8_slice_bound_verifier_error.gx
//     [2, 3]
//     fusion: 2 of 8 attempted regions fused, 6 blocked
//       1x define_function (shared body): Compilation error: Verifier errors:
//          - inst10 (v17, v18 = call fn4(v14, v9, v6, v15, v16)):
//            arg 2 (v6) has type i8, expected i64
//
// The same program with an `i64:1` bound fuses cleanly, and a bytes
// twin (`let i = u8:1; b[i]`) produces the identical verifier error.
//
// Root cause: two sites hand a narrow operand's CLIF payload straight to
// a helper whose signature declares i64.
//   - fusion/emit/nodes.rs:1241 — `graphix_bytes_index(v: TagValue, i: i64)`
//     (emit_helpers.rs:960) called as
//     `call(helper, &[bcv.disc, bcv.payload, idx_cv.payload])`.
//   - fusion/emit/nodes.rs:1333 — `graphix_array_slice(src, start: i64,
//     end: i64, flags: i64)` (emit_helpers.rs:982) receiving
//     `Ok(cv.payload)` from the `emit_bound` closure.
// Both already know the operand's PrimType, so they can widen.
//
// Fix: widen at both call sites — `widen_to_i64(cx.b, payload, prim)`,
// the helper the init-loop's count already uses
// (emit/scaffold.rs ~902) — signed-extending signed prims and
// zero-extending unsigned ones. Two one-line changes.
//
// Worth noting beyond the coverage loss: a cranelift VERIFIER error is
// not a clean de-fuse. It surfaces as a `define_function` failure that
// discards a whole shared body, so a single narrow index anywhere in a
// region takes the entire region (and its transitive callees) down with
// it. A grep for other unwidened helper operands is warranted — the two
// found here were both in index/slice bound positions.
let result = {
  let a = [i64:1, i64:2, i64:3];
  let i = u8:1;
  let s = a[i..];
  s
};
println("[result]")
